---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const a31 = requireSection('A3.1');
const a32 = requireSection('A3.2');
const a33 = requireSection('A3.3');
const a34 = requireSection('A3.4');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    A3 starts with relational databases because they give the clearest foundation for structure, integrity,
    and query logic. The chapter then moves from design into SQL, and finally into higher-level choices
    about scale, analytics, and distributed storage.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Keep the sequence clear: model the data first, then build tables from that model, then query and update
      the data, and only after that evaluate HL alternatives such as warehouses and distributed databases.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a31}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.1.1"
    summary="Explain what makes a database relational, why that model is useful, and where its limitations begin to matter."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A relational database stores data in related tables. Each table represents one type of thing, each row
      represents one record, and each column represents one attribute. The model becomes powerful when keys
      connect tables without forcing the same information to be repeated everywhere.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-relational-database-features">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Element</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Primary key</td>
          <td>Uniquely identifies each record in a table</td>
          <td>Prevents ambiguity when retrieving or linking data</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Foreign key</td>
          <td>References a primary key in another table</td>
          <td>Creates relationships and supports referential integrity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Composite key</td>
          <td>Uses more than one attribute to identify a record</td>
          <td>Useful when one field alone is not unique</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Relationships</td>
          <td>Connect tables through keys</td>
          <td>Allow complex systems to be modeled without storing everything in one table</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Relational databases are strong when data is highly structured, integrity matters, and users need
      reliable querying across related tables. They are weaker when the schema changes constantly, when the
      data is heavily hierarchical or unstructured, or when the system must scale to very large, varied data
      sets with minimal schema rigidity.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-linking-two-tables">Worked example: linking two tables</h3>
    <p>
      A school library might store book details in one table and loan details in another. The book identifier
      appears once as a primary key in <code>Book</code> and then again as a foreign key in
      <code>Loan</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Table</th>
          <th scope="col">Important fields</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Book</td>
          <td><code>BookID</code>, <code>Title</code>, <code>Author</code></td>
          <td>Stores information about each book once</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Loan</td>
          <td><code>LoanID</code>, <code>BookID</code>, <code>BorrowerID</code>, <code>DueDate</code></td>
          <td>Stores each borrowing event without repeating full book details</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      This design reduces duplication and makes updates more reliable. A title change happens in one place,
      not across every loan record.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a32}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.2.1, A3.2.2, A3.2.3, A3.2.4"
    summary="Move from abstract schema design into ERDs, data types, and table construction for a clean relational model."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Database design works in layers. First decide what the system needs to represent, then define the
      entities and relationships, then choose suitable data types, and only then construct the tables.
      Skipping that order usually produces awkward tables and unreliable data.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-schemas-erds-and-data-types">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Schema level</th>
          <th scope="col">Main question</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical content</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Conceptual schema</td>
          <td>What needs to be represented?</td>
          <td>Entities, broad relationships, business meaning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Logical schema</td>
          <td>How will the relational structure work?</td>
          <td>Tables, attributes, keys, constraints</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Physical schema</td>
          <td>How is it implemented in a DBMS?</td>
          <td>Storage choices, indexes, DBMS-specific details</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      ERDs help at the conceptual and logical stages because they force you to identify entities clearly and
      define relationships through cardinality and modality. A one-to-many relationship tells you that one
      record on one side may connect to many on the other side; modality tells you whether that connection is
      optional or required.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Data type</th>
          <th scope="col">Good use</th>
          <th scope="col">Risk of a poor choice</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>INT</code></td>
          <td>Identifiers or whole-number counts</td>
          <td>Text-like values such as phone numbers lose formatting or leading zeroes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>VARCHAR</code></td>
          <td>Names, titles, codes with variable length</td>
          <td>Numeric comparison becomes unreliable if true numbers are stored as text</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>DATE</code></td>
          <td>Deadlines, enrolment dates, booking dates</td>
          <td>Text dates are harder to validate, sort, and compare</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>DECIMAL</code></td>
          <td>Prices or measured values that require precision</td>
          <td>Using integers may force awkward scaling rules</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-from-erd-to-tables">Worked example: from ERD to tables</h3>
    <p>
      Suppose the system tracks clubs and student membership. A student can join many clubs, and a club can
      contain many students. That is a many-to-many relationship, so it cannot be represented directly in one
      relational link.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Table</th>
          <th scope="col">Key fields</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it exists</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Student</td>
          <td><code>StudentID</code>, <code>StudentName</code></td>
          <td>Stores each student once</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Club</td>
          <td><code>ClubID</code>, <code>ClubName</code>, <code>TeacherID</code></td>
          <td>Stores each club once</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Membership</td>
          <td><code>StudentID</code>, <code>ClubID</code></td>
          <td>Acts as the linking table for the many-to-many relationship</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.2.5, A3.2.6, A3.2.7"
    summary="Use normal forms to reduce redundancy, normalize designs to 3NF, and then evaluate when controlled denormalization may be justified."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Normalization improves integrity by removing avoidable repetition and dependency problems. In exam
      terms, you need to explain what changes between normal forms and then construct a design that reaches
      3NF for a realistic scenario.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-normalization-and-3nf">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Normal form</th>
          <th scope="col">Main rule</th>
          <th scope="col">Problem it removes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1NF</td>
          <td>Values must be atomic and repeating groups removed</td>
          <td>Multiple values stored in one field</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2NF</td>
          <td>Non-key attributes must depend on the whole key</td>
          <td>Partial dependency on part of a composite key</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3NF</td>
          <td>Non-key attributes must depend only on the key</td>
          <td>Transitive dependency between non-key attributes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-normalizing-a-booking-table">Worked example: normalizing a booking table</h3>
    <p>
      Imagine one flat table stores <code>BookingID</code>, <code>StudentName</code>,
      <code>ClubTitle</code>, <code>TeacherName</code>, and <code>RoomName</code>. If one teacher runs
      many clubs, teacher and room information is repeated across many rows.
    </p>
    <p>
      A 3NF design separates the repeated ideas into distinct tables such as <code>Student</code>,
      <code>Club</code>, <code>Teacher</code>, and <code>Booking</code>. The result is easier to update
      correctly because a room change or teacher-name correction happens once.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Evaluation rule">
      <p>
        Denormalization is not automatically poor design. It may be justified when read performance or
        reporting simplicity matters more than perfect reduction of redundancy, but the trade-off is extra
        duplication and a greater risk of inconsistent data.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a33}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.3.1, A3.3.2, A3.3.3"
    summary="Use SQL structure definitions, joined queries, and update statements to define and manipulate relational data."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      SQL is the working language of relational databases. The syllabus expects you to distinguish between
      language types, construct queries between tables, and explain how updates change stored data.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-ddl-dml-and-joins">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Language type</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical commands</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>DDL</td>
          <td>Define or alter structure</td>
          <td><code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>DML</td>
          <td>Retrieve or modify stored data</td>
          <td><code>SELECT</code>, <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-sql">CREATE TABLE Department (
  DepartmentID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  DepartmentName VARCHAR(40)
);

CREATE TABLE Employee (
  EmployeeID INT PRIMARY KEY,
  DepartmentID INT,
  LastName VARCHAR(40),
  Salary DECIMAL(10,2),
  FOREIGN KEY (DepartmentID) REFERENCES Department(DepartmentID)
);</code></pre>

    <h3 id="worked-example-querying-two-related-tables">Worked example: querying two related tables</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">SELECT Employee.LastName, Department.DepartmentName
FROM Employee
JOIN Department ON Employee.DepartmentID = Department.DepartmentID
WHERE Department.DepartmentName LIKE 'Sc%'
ORDER BY Employee.LastName ASC;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This query joins two tables, filters matching rows, uses pattern matching, and orders the result. That
      is the kind of constructed query the syllabus expects, even though exact SQL syntax may vary slightly
      between systems.
    </p>

    <pre><code class="language-sql">INSERT INTO Employee (EmployeeID, DepartmentID, LastName, Salary)
VALUES (14, 3, 'Khan', 42000);

UPDATE Employee
SET Salary = 43500
WHERE EmployeeID = 14;

DELETE FROM Employee
WHERE EmployeeID = 14;</code></pre>

    <p>
      When indexed columns are updated, the database may also need to update the index structure, so heavy
      modification can have performance costs as well as correctness implications.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.3.4, A3.3.5, A3.3.6"
    summary="HL extends SQL work into aggregation, database views, and transactions that preserve integrity through ACID."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Higher level work shifts from retrieving or changing single rows to building summaries, controlling
      access through views, and managing transactions so that the database remains valid even when operations
      fail part way through.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-aggregation-views-and-transactions">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Representative example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Aggregate function</td>
          <td>Summarize many rows into one result</td>
          <td><code>COUNT</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>SUM</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>View</td>
          <td>Present data in a controlled or simplified way</td>
          <td>Virtual view or materialized snapshot view</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transaction</td>
          <td>Treat several operations as one unit of work</td>
          <td><code>BEGIN TRANSACTION</code>, <code>COMMIT</code>, <code>ROLLBACK</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <pre><code class="language-sql">SELECT DepartmentID, AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DepartmentID
HAVING AVG(Salary) &gt; 35000;</code></pre>

    <p>
      Views help with security and simplification because users do not always need direct access to every
      table and every attribute. A virtual view stores the query definition; a materialized view stores a
      snapshot result for faster repeated access.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-acid-in-action">Worked example: ACID in action</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-sql">BEGIN TRANSACTION;

UPDATE Inventory
SET Quantity = Quantity - 1
WHERE ItemID = 27;

INSERT INTO Orders (OrderID, ItemID, CustomerID)
VALUES (9041, 27, 301);

COMMIT;</code></pre>
    <p>
      If the insert fails after the stock level has already changed, the transaction should be rolled back.
      Atomicity prevents a half-complete update; consistency keeps rules valid; isolation prevents conflicts
      between simultaneous work; durability preserves committed changes.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a34}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.4.1"
    summary="Outline alternative database models by matching structure, scale, and use case rather than treating them as interchangeable replacements."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Relational databases are not the only way to store data. Different models exist because different
      problems demand different structures, performance characteristics, and operational trade-offs.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-alternative-database-models">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Key strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Key limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>NoSQL</td>
          <td>Large, varied, fast-changing data</td>
          <td>Flexible schema and scalability</td>
          <td>Less natural for strict relational integrity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloud database</td>
          <td>Managed web or enterprise deployment</td>
          <td>Elastic scaling and managed availability</td>
          <td>Provider dependence and reduced infrastructure control</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Spatial database</td>
          <td>Geographic and mapping queries</td>
          <td>Native support for coordinates and geometry</td>
          <td>Requires specialized data types and querying</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>In-memory database</td>
          <td>Real-time access and low-latency analytics</td>
          <td>Very fast read and write operations</td>
          <td>Higher cost and volatility concerns if persistence is weak</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-a-database-model">Worked example: choosing a database model</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Strongest model</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Social-media content with changing post metadata</td>
          <td>NoSQL document database</td>
          <td>Flexible schema handles semi-structured records well</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>School mapping tool with route and area data</td>
          <td>Spatial database</td>
          <td>Coordinates, regions, and location queries are central</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Session caching for a high-traffic online service</td>
          <td>In-memory key-value database</td>
          <td>Low latency matters more than complex relational structure</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.4.2, A3.4.3"
    summary="Explain what data warehouses are for and how OLAP and data mining support business intelligence."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A data warehouse is designed for analysis rather than day-to-day transaction processing. It gathers
      historical data from multiple sources so that trends, patterns, and comparisons can be studied over
      time.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-data-warehousing-and-bi">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Warehouse characteristic</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Append-only</td>
          <td>Keeps historical versions so change over time can be analysed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Subject-oriented</td>
          <td>Organizes data around themes such as finance, sales, or operations</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Integrated</td>
          <td>Combines data from multiple sources into one analytical view</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Time-variant</td>
          <td>Supports trends, comparisons, and historical reporting</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Non-volatile</td>
          <td>Data is stable for analysis rather than constantly rewritten</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Optimized for query performance</td>
          <td>Supports fast retrieval of analytical summaries</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      OLAP helps users explore summarized data from multiple dimensions such as time, product, or region.
      Data mining goes further by finding patterns in large data sets using techniques such as classification,
      clustering, regression, or association discovery.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-business-intelligence-workflow">Worked example: business intelligence workflow</h3>
    <p>
      A retailer wants to understand falling weekend sales. The warehouse stores several years of sales data.
      OLAP can compare region, week, and product category quickly. Data mining can then look for patterns
      such as products frequently bought together or customer groups whose buying behaviour has changed.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A3.4.4"
    summary="Describe distributed databases through the features needed to keep data available, consistent, and usable across several nodes."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A distributed database presents one usable system view even though the data is stored across multiple
      sites. The challenge is not only storage location; it is maintaining reliable behaviour while data,
      queries, and transactions are spread across the network.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-distributed-database-features">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Feature</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Concurrency control</td>
          <td>Prevents conflicting updates when several nodes access the same data at once</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Data consistency</td>
          <td>Keeps multiple copies synchronized to a correct state</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Replication</td>
          <td>Improves availability and resilience by storing copies across nodes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Fault tolerance</td>
          <td>Allows service to continue despite hardware, software, or network failure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Location and distribution transparency</td>
          <td>Lets users work with the system without needing to know where data physically sits</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scalability and partitioning</td>
          <td>Support growth in data volume and workload by spreading storage and requests</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="recap" label="Transaction link">
      <p>
        ACID still matters here. In a distributed setting, atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability
        are harder to preserve because more than one node may be involved in the same transaction.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
